WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.530 --> 00:00:05.550
With Hollywood cranking out blockbuster fare
at a staggering rate, and arthouse cinema

00:00:05.550 --> 00:00:10.320
fully coming into its own, the '90s were an
excitingly mixed bag at the multiplex.

00:00:10.480 --> 00:00:15.719
This combination of high and low art carried
over into video stores too, inspiring movie

00:00:15.720 --> 00:00:21.160
buffs to scour shelves for both big-budget
bonanzas and obscure festival hits.

00:00:21.340 --> 00:00:26.290
Sadly, those searches often proved fruitless,
leaving some of the decade's best films largely

00:00:26.290 --> 00:00:27.620
unseen by the masses.

00:00:27.810 --> 00:00:32.520
Here are a few '90s gems that are absolutely
worthy of your attention — if you can track

00:00:32.520 --> 00:00:33.600
them down, that is.

00:00:34.180 --> 00:00:36.080
Twin Peaks: Fire Walk With Me

00:00:36.420 --> 00:00:42.450
No single show changed the landscape of television
in the '90s more than David Lynch's supernatural

00:00:42.450 --> 00:00:44.380
murder mystery Twin Peaks.

00:00:44.420 --> 00:00:48.680
Even after a game-changing first season, the
show was unceremoniously cancelled in the

00:00:48.680 --> 00:00:51.240
midst of its wildly uneven second season.

00:00:51.380 --> 00:00:56.300
Once the show ended, fans were delighted to
find out that Lynch still had Twin Peaks tales

00:00:56.320 --> 00:00:57.020
to tell.

00:00:57.340 --> 00:01:01.239
One of those tales — that of Laura Palmer's
last week on Earth — got the big screen

00:01:01.240 --> 00:01:03.780
treatment in Twin Peaks: Fire Walk With Me.

00:01:04.120 --> 00:01:09.080
Free from the creative shackles of television,
Lynch used the chance to craft his most complex,

00:01:09.080 --> 00:01:10.920
uncompromising film to date.

00:01:11.160 --> 00:01:16.460
Sadly, even the most devoted of Twin Peaks'
fanbase had trouble connecting with Fire Walk

00:01:16.480 --> 00:01:18.800
With Me's artsy eccentricities.

00:01:18.909 --> 00:01:23.380
The film proved divisive amongst critics,
all but ignored in its theatrical release,

00:01:23.380 --> 00:01:26.780
and unfairly doomed to a sort of purgatory
in the process.

00:01:26.950 --> 00:01:31.880
For all of its quirks, Fire Walk With Me stands
as a creative high point for Lynch, breathlessly

00:01:31.880 --> 00:01:36.310
combining his penchant for experimentation
with his trademarked biting commentary on

00:01:36.310 --> 00:01:37.360
the American dream.

00:01:37.660 --> 00:01:41.340
Though the film has had its defenders over
the years, and has long held the title of

00:01:41.340 --> 00:01:45.240
"cult classic," it still feels like not enough
people have actually seen it.

00:01:46.340 --> 00:01:46.980
Chaplin

00:01:47.920 --> 00:01:53.080
Before Robert Downey Jr. put Marvel on the
map with his now iconic turn in Iron Man,

00:01:53.340 --> 00:01:57.659
Richard Attenborough tapped him to star in
his Charlie Chaplin bio-pic, appropriately

00:01:57.660 --> 00:01:58.780
titled Chaplin.

00:01:59.070 --> 00:02:03.420
Lucky for Attenborough — and for film buffs
everywhere — Downey Jr. turned in the finest

00:02:03.420 --> 00:02:07.880
performance of his young career, landing RDJ
his first Oscar nomination.

00:02:08.060 --> 00:02:11.640
But Attenborough's film is more than just
a showcase for Downey Jr.'s talent.

00:02:11.980 --> 00:02:17.340
Though it didn't fare well with critics who
lamented its 143-minute runtime and overall

00:02:17.349 --> 00:02:22.700
lack of ingenuity, Chaplin remains a lovingly
crafted, in-depth look behind the curtain

00:02:22.700 --> 00:02:24.780
of one of cinema's greatest innovators.

00:02:25.000 --> 00:02:29.680
The film is likely to inspire viewers to go
back and explore Chaplin's own mind-blowing

00:02:29.680 --> 00:02:34.540
body of work as well, not to mention the mostly
terrific work Downey Jr. has delivered in

00:02:34.540 --> 00:02:36.420
the years since Chaplin's release.

00:02:36.980 --> 00:02:38.020
Red Rock West

00:02:38.640 --> 00:02:43.500
Things have certainly gotten away from Nicolas
Cage over the past decade or so, but throughout

00:02:43.510 --> 00:02:48.319
the '90s he was still deemed an exciting,
supremely talented actor, the sort who could

00:02:48.320 --> 00:02:51.830
handle big-budget fare but still wow in micro-budget
indies.

00:02:51.900 --> 00:02:56.380
So it came as no surprise that he'd follow
one of his biggest box office hits — Honeymoon

00:02:56.380 --> 00:03:01.160
In Vegas — by starring as a wayward drifter
in John Dahl's Red Rock West.

00:03:01.460 --> 00:03:04.770
Don't worry if you've never heard of the brilliant
neo-noir thriller.

00:03:04.770 --> 00:03:08.700
The film's been all but lost to history since
its release — which is a shame, because

00:03:08.700 --> 00:03:13.670
Dahl's nimble tale of small-town swindling
and mistaken identity is just as slick today

00:03:13.670 --> 00:03:15.840
as it was back in 1996.

00:03:16.120 --> 00:03:21.000
It also happens to feature one of Cage's most
understated and effecting performances.

00:03:21.220 --> 00:03:25.900
With any luck, the aging actor might one day
remember that, sometimes, less actually is

00:03:25.900 --> 00:03:28.140
more — especially when bees are involved.

00:03:29.380 --> 00:03:30.440
"Not not the bees.

00:03:30.900 --> 00:03:31.780
"not the bees."

00:03:32.040 --> 00:03:33.040
Ah."

00:03:34.760 --> 00:03:35.260
"My eyes.

00:03:35.600 --> 00:03:36.320
My eyes.

00:03:36.780 --> 00:03:37.560
Ah"

00:03:38.140 --> 00:03:38.680
Dick

00:03:39.540 --> 00:03:44.340
Back in 1999, a political satire titled Dick
should've gotten a lot more attention than

00:03:44.340 --> 00:03:44.840
it did.

00:03:44.960 --> 00:03:49.720
Sadly, in the almost two decades since the
comedy's release, the film still hasn't found

00:03:49.720 --> 00:03:50.480
its audience.

00:03:50.660 --> 00:03:53.880
With any luck, however, the current political
climate might change that.

00:03:53.940 --> 00:03:58.960
After all, the girl-power-centric story of
political upheaval carries as much weight

00:03:58.960 --> 00:04:01.080
today as it might have in the year it was
set.

00:04:01.280 --> 00:04:06.219
The fact that Dick also features winning performances
from Kirsten Dunst, Michelle Williams, and

00:04:06.220 --> 00:04:10.940
Dan Hedaya as a brilliant Nixon caricature
makes it even more worthy of notice.

00:04:11.040 --> 00:04:16.000
Full of insightful stoner-styled comedy, and
featuring a terrific supporting cast of gifted

00:04:16.000 --> 00:04:20.820
comedians, Dick is a must-see that too many
moviegoers missed the first time around.

00:04:21.220 --> 00:04:23.380
Ghost Dog: The Way of the Samurai

00:04:23.700 --> 00:04:30.039
Loosely adapted from Jean-Pierre Melville's
New Wave classic, Le Samurai, 1999's Ghost

00:04:30.039 --> 00:04:35.080
Dog: The Way of the Samurai sees notoriously
experimental director Jim Jarmusch at his

00:04:35.080 --> 00:04:36.140
most accessible.

00:04:36.400 --> 00:04:41.360
Whereas Le Samurai saw its titular silent
assassin skulking around the back alleys of

00:04:41.360 --> 00:04:46.700
Paris, Forest Whitaker's eponymous Ghost Dog
calls the mean streets of Brooklyn home.

00:04:46.780 --> 00:04:52.220
Jarmusch bolstered the film's spiritual insights
by frequently quoting Samurai philosophy and

00:04:52.220 --> 00:04:57.510
backed his sword-swinging killer's gritty,
urban journey with a head-nodding original

00:04:57.510 --> 00:04:59.840
score from Wu Tang Clan's RZA.

00:04:59.880 --> 00:05:05.600
Combining ancient warrior wisdom with a stylish
hip-hop vibe, Jarmusch spins an engrossing

00:05:05.610 --> 00:05:10.639
and deeply satisfying story of modern-day
honor amongst thieves that still resonates

00:05:10.640 --> 00:05:12.120
with film fanatics today.

00:05:12.920 --> 00:05:14.220
Stir of Echoes

00:05:15.120 --> 00:05:20.280
The ripple effect from The Sixth Sense's breakout
success is still being felt in Hollywood today;

00:05:20.420 --> 00:05:24.460
in other words, it seems like every modern
horror film comes prepackaged with its own

00:05:24.460 --> 00:05:25.940
flashy, twist ending.

00:05:26.260 --> 00:05:31.220
Now, imagine that you released a haunting
horror film rife with unsettling imagery — and,

00:05:31.220 --> 00:05:36.760
yes, a shocking twist — a mere month after
The Sixth Sense changed the horror game.

00:05:36.840 --> 00:05:41.640
Even if your film was just as clever and scary
as M. Night Shyamalan's masterpiece, it'd

00:05:41.640 --> 00:05:46.060
be virtually impossible to match those Sixth
Sense-sized expectations.

00:05:46.380 --> 00:05:49.800
Such was the case with David Koepp's Stir
of Echoes.

00:05:49.980 --> 00:05:54.520
Following the trials and tribulations of a
working-class dad who starts to experience

00:05:54.520 --> 00:05:59.680
terrifying visions after a hypnosis hiccup,
Koepp's film is often just as smart and creepy

00:05:59.680 --> 00:06:00.700
as The Sixth Sense.

00:06:00.970 --> 00:06:06.520
At times, Stir of Echoes even bests Shyamalan's
flick on both fronts, but it also features

00:06:06.520 --> 00:06:11.530
a child who can speak to dead people, so yeah,
you can see the problem with its being released

00:06:11.530 --> 00:06:15.400
after Haley Joel Osment uttered those now
iconic words.

00:06:16.180 --> 00:06:17.680
"I see dead people."

00:06:18.160 --> 00:06:22.880
Still, Stir of Echoes is far from a cheap
knock off, and horror fans unfamiliar with

00:06:22.880 --> 00:06:25.800
the unsung flick owe it to themselves to give
it a shot.

00:06:26.420 --> 00:06:27.520
Thanks for watching!

